  <h1>IgG vs IgM Antibody Test</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> IgM appears first after infection and indicates a recent or active immune response; IgG appears later, persists long-term and indicates past exposure or immunity. Together they help distinguish recent from past infection.</p>

  <h2>Comparison table</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>IgM</th><th>IgG</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Appears</th><td>First (days after infection)</td><td>Later (weeks)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Persists</th><td>Weeks to months</td><td>Months to years</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Indicates</th><td>Recent / active infection</td><td>Past exposure / immunity</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Example use</th><td>Acute dengue, HAV IgM</td><td>Rubella immunity, past exposure</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What each marker indicates</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>IgM positive:</strong> likely recent infection — but cross-reactivity can cause false positives.</li>
    <li><strong>IgG positive:</strong> past exposure or immunity — may not indicate current infection.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Testing window</h2>
  <p>IgM is most useful in the acute phase; IgG becomes reliable later. Paired or combined IgM/IgG testing widens the window.</p>

  <h2>Strengths</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>IgM:</strong> early indicator of recent infection.</li>
    <li><strong>IgG:</strong> immunity assessment (e.g., rubella, varicella); persists for monitoring.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>IgM:</strong> cross-reactivity, false positives.</li>
    <li><strong>IgG:</strong> cannot confirm active infection alone; timing-dependent.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>False positive / negative considerations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>False positive IgM:</strong> cross-reactivity with related pathogens.</li>
    <li><strong>False negative IgG:</strong> testing before seroconversion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Confirmatory testing</h2>
  <p>Serology is supportive; confirm active infection with antigen or molecular (PCR) testing where applicable.</p>

  <h2>Related pages</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/comparisons/antigen-vs-antibody/">Antigen vs Antibody</a></li>
    <li><a href="/comparisons/dengue-ns1-vs-igg-igm/">Dengue NS1 vs IgM/IgG</a></li>
  </ul>

  
  <h2>Which test to choose</h2>
  <p>Choose <strong>IgM</strong> to detect a recent infection; choose <strong>IgG</strong> for past-exposure or immunity assessment. Combined IgM+IgG covers both phases.</p>
